  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Safe and Friendly Spaces with Water and Sanitation for Children, Adolescents and Their Families on the Move in the Dominican Republic</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Migration between Haiti and the Dominican Republic is the most active in the Caribbean. In recent years, the migratory flow, mostly irregular, has increased due to the humanitarian and security crisis in Haiti. Faced with this situation, the Dominican Republic has tightened its immigration control policy, deporting an average of 20,000 people per month since 2023, including an estimated 815 children. With the increase in deportations, the Migration Interdiction Centers of the General Directorate of Migration lack the facilities to guarantee a safe and friendly space, with access to drinking water and sanitation, for children and adolescents and their families on the move. The International Organization for Migration (IOM) and the United Nations Children&#x2019;s Fund access the centers in the border area, Santo Domingo and Santiago on a daily basis to provide humanitarian assistance and mitigate the net impact of detention on the physical and mental wellbeing of migrants. The main objective of this project is to help children, adolescents and their families on the move or affected by migration benefit from water, sanitation and hygiene services as well as safe and friendly spaces in migration interdiction centers.</narrative>
  </description>
